---
description: |
  Verification half of the record_integrity lane: recomputes every stored
  record's attestation fingerprint over the canonical serialization and
  walks the prev_event chains, emitting a Detection Finding (2004) on any
  break. Input is the stored-shape record (post-cast, attested) as read
  back from the query store; the live lanes read it via from_clickhouse,
  the package tests feed the same shape from fixtures. Records without an
  attestation predate the chain epoch and are skipped (docs/issues/
  tenzir-record-integrity-attestations.md: pre-stamping rows are out of
  scope). A mid-chain record whose chain link is absent is the documented
  chain-restart case (the attest operator warned at write time when the
  node lost its in-memory chain state) and is not a break: the
  per-record fingerprint recompute still catches content tampering
  across restarts. Detecting the absent link structurally (the attest
  stamping contract: two attestations on linked records, one on
  restarts) is load-bearing on homogeneous batches — one class table,
  every node's verify lane — where schema unification types the
  out-of-bounds link access through the siblings' element schema and
  the string-typed fingerprint path reads as the empty string instead
  of null, which would flag a false chain_link_mismatch on every
  post-restart record (probed on the netflow node's fixture,
  v6.19.1).
  Chains are walked per attestation_list[0].chain_uid — the OCSF chain
  identifier (objects/attestation.md: "querying events whose
  attestation.chain_uid matches retrieves the full chain") — which is
  the case spine on the investigations plane and the node identity on
  the detection plane. The finding's dressing — product, title, uid
  prefix, and the scope word in the description — is the caller's plane
  identity. The record itself is schema-native class 2004: severity 4
  (the Grafana findings gate) and finding_info (singular, required by
  Detection Finding — the finding_info.title / finding_info.uid typed
  columns and the alert rule's finding-label coalesce read it), so the
  scheduled verify pipelines can ocsf_derive + ocsf_cast the finding
  and write it into ocsf.Detection_Finding to ride the existing alert
  rails. `windowed` covers bounded read-backs (rolling-window
  detection walks): the walk cannot see the predecessor of the first
  record it visits, so that record's link checks are skipped — its
  per-record fingerprint recompute still runs. `link` selects the chain
  shape the attest operator stamped: "caller" (investigations — the
  single attestation carries prev_event and its fingerprint covers it)
  or "stream" (detection — attestation_list[0] is the record
  fingerprint hashed without prev_event, the chain link rides
  un-hashed in attestation_list[1]; the recompute rebuilds
  attestation_list[0] with the link stripped from the input, and the
  link integrity is enforced by the adjacency check itself).
args:
  named:
    - name: product
      type: string
      description: The finding's metadata.product.name — the plane
        identity (homelab-investigations / homelab-detection).
    - name: title
      type: string
      description: The finding_info title ("Investigations record chain
        break" / "Detection record chain break").
    - name: uid_prefix
      type: string
      description: The finding_info uid prefix — the uid reads
        prefix, literal -attest-, then the chain uid.
    - name: scope_word
      type: string
      description: The chain descriptor in the finding description —
        "case" (investigations) or "chain" (detection).
    - name: windowed
      type: bool
      description: True for bounded read-backs — the first record of
        each chain in the walk may reference a predecessor outside the
        read window, so its link checks are skipped. False walks the
        whole store (investigations) — a chain head referencing a
        predecessor the walk never saw is a deletion.
    - name: link
      type: string
      default: caller
      description: caller — the investigations shape (attestation_list[0]
        carries prev_event inside its hash). stream — the detection shape
        (attestation_list[0] is the record fingerprint; the chain link
        rides un-hashed in attestation_list[1]).
---

// One pass in time order per chain: bounded read-back, so sort first and
// the group's inner pipeline sees each key's events in that order
// (reference/operators/lag.md — "The operator follows input order within
// its pipeline"). The metadata.uid tie-break makes the order a
// deterministic total order: the detection plane mints a uid on every
// archived record precisely so same-second stamps (k8s-audit volume)
// sort reproducibly here — the stamp side sorts on the same key pair,
// and a tie left to input order would differ between the archive pass
// and the read-back walk (a false chain_link_mismatch).
where attestation_list? != null
sort time, metadata.uid
group attestation_list[0].chain_uid {
  // ---- the recompute (must see a clean record) -----------------------------
  // Nothing has been written into the record yet — no walk state, no
  // plumbing fields — so the spread below hashes the stored record with
  // the attestation rebuilt exactly as the attest operator assembled it
  // pre-fingerprint (OCSF rule, vendored objects/attestation.md: the
  // hash covers the entire event including the attestation's own
  // authority_uid, chain_uid, and prev_event, and excludes only the
  // attestation's fingerprint and signatures). prev_event rides absent
  // on genesis records: a null field would serialize differently from a
  // missing one and break the recompute. Stream-mode records carry the
  // un-hashed chain link in attestation_list[1]: it is stripped from the
  // hash input because at stamp time attestation_list[0] was hashed over
  // the record before the link existed.
  _recomputed = hash_sha256(print_json(({
    ...this,
    attestation_list: [
      {
        authority_uid: attestation_list[0].authority_uid,
        chain_uid: attestation_list[0].chain_uid,
        prev_event: attestation_list[0].prev_event,
      } if $link == "caller" and attestation_list[0]?.prev_event? != null else {
        authority_uid: attestation_list[0].authority_uid,
        chain_uid: attestation_list[0].chain_uid,
      },
    ],
    // The stream stamp nulled the caller's prev_tail (the batch-seam
    // field) in its hash input the same way — the stored record carries
    // no prev_tail, so nulling it here reproduces the stamp-side input
    // byte-for-byte. The caller shape never sees the field at all.
    prev_tail: null,
  }).sort())) if $link == "stream" else hash_sha256(print_json(({
    ...this,
    attestation_list: [
      {
        authority_uid: attestation_list[0].authority_uid,
        chain_uid: attestation_list[0].chain_uid,
        prev_event: attestation_list[0].prev_event,
      } if attestation_list[0]?.prev_event? != null else {
        authority_uid: attestation_list[0].authority_uid,
        chain_uid: attestation_list[0].chain_uid,
      },
    ],
  }).sort()))

  // The chain link: the caller shape keeps it inside
  // attestation_list[0]; the stream shape rides it un-hashed in
  // attestation_list[1] (absent on genesis and restart records). The
  // length guard on the stream shape is load-bearing: on a homogeneous
  // batch — one class table, the shape every node's verify lane reads —
  // schema unification lets the out-of-bounds attestation_list[1]? on
  // a stripped-link record resolve through the linked siblings'
  // element schema, and the string-typed prev_event fingerprint path
  // then reads as "" (the typed-null string) instead of null, flagging
  // a false chain_link_mismatch on the documented restart shape
  // (probed on the netflow node's fixture, v6.19.1). length() is the
  // structural truth the padding cannot touch: attest stamps exactly
  // one attestation on genesis/restart records, two on linked ones.
  _link_att = attestation_list[0] if $link == "caller" else (attestation_list[1]? if length(attestation_list) > 1 else null)

  // ---- the walk state -------------------------------------------------------
  // The predecessor's tail in this chain's time order — written AFTER the
  // recompute so it never enters the hash input. Read-only walk state
  // over complete records: no write, no ordering hazards. The fp is the
  // record fingerprint (attestation_list[0]) in both link shapes — that
  // is what the chain links reference.
  lag value={
    uid: metadata.uid,
    type_uid: type_uid,
    fp: attestation_list[0]?.fingerprint?.value?,
  }, into=_prev

  // ---- the checks -----------------------------------------------------------
  // The stored prev_event fingerprint for this record, null on genesis
  // and on restart records. The stream-mode branch is a structural
  // normalization, and it is load-bearing: attest stamps TWO
  // attestations on linked records and exactly ONE on genesis/restart
  // records, so a length-1 list is the documented no-link shape — and
  // on a homogeneous batch (one class table, the shape every node's
  // verify lane reads) schema unification lets the siblings' element
  // schema type the out-of-bounds link access, so the string-typed
  // fingerprint path would read as "" (the typed-null string) instead
  // of null and flag a false chain_link_mismatch on every post-restart
  // record (probed on the netflow node's fixture, v6.19.1). A
  // kept-but-tampered link (length 2, the fingerprint edited to ""
  // post-stamp) takes the else branch and still mismatches loudly.
  // NB: the guard must be the explicit if-else form — a bare
  // `x = null if c` is an if-expression with an implicit null else,
  // and would null the stored fingerprint on EVERY record.
  _stored_prev_fp = null if $link == "stream" and length(attestation_list) < 2 else _link_att?.prev_event?.fingerprint?.value?
  _break = null
  // Content mutation: the recomputed fingerprint differs from the stored
  // one — the record (or its stored attestation) was altered after
  // stamping. This is the check that survives chain restarts.
  if _recomputed != attestation_list[0]?.fingerprint?.value? {
    _break = "fingerprint_mismatch"
  } else {
    if _prev? == null {
      // The first record of this chain the walk sees. Unwindowed: a
      // stored prev_event with no walked predecessor means the chain
      // head references a record the walk never saw — deletion of the
      // chain's earlier records (a first record with prev_event can
      // never have been stamped — its predecessor existed at stamp
      // time). Windowed: the predecessor may simply live outside the
      // read window — not a break.
      if _stored_prev_fp? != null and not $windowed {
        _break = "chain_head_missing"
      }
    } else if _stored_prev_fp? != null and _stored_prev_fp != _prev?.fp? {
      // Broken link: prev_event does not reference the immediately
      // preceding record of this chain — deletion, reorder, or splice.
      // In the stream shape this adjacency check is also the chain
      // link's integrity check: the un-hashed link attestation carries
      // no fingerprint of its own for a recompute, so this is where a
      // tampered or spliced link surfaces.
      _break = "chain_link_mismatch"
    }
    // A mid-chain record with no chain link is the documented
    // restart case — the attest operator warned at write time — and no
    // break.
  }

  // ---- findings out, intact records silent ----------------------------------
  // where false drops the event (a bare drop is a field-list operator);
  // the broken record's fields survive only inside the finding's unmapped
  // evidence block. A chain-tail deletion (no successor references it)
  // stays invisible to this walk — the honest limit of an unanchored
  // hash chain, documented in the datalake README.
  if _break == null {
    where false
  } else {
    this = {
      metadata: {
        uid: f"attest-break-{attestation_list[0].chain_uid}-{metadata.uid}",
        version: "1.9.0",
        correlation_uid: attestation_list[0].chain_uid,
        product: {
          name: $product,
          vendor_name: "apocrathia",
          version: "0.1.0",
        },
      },
      time: now(),
      category_uid: 2,
      class_uid: 2004,
      activity_id: 1,
      severity_id: 4,
      status_id: 1,
      type_uid: 200401,
      // finding_info (singular): class 2004 requires it, and the lake's
      // finding_info.title / finding_info.uid typed columns plus the
      // Grafana findings rule's finding-label coalesce read it — the
      // verify findings ride the same rails the sigma findings do.
      finding_info: {
        uid: f"{$uid_prefix}-attest-{attestation_list[0].chain_uid}",
        title: $title,
        // The broken record's time, not now(): the finding is anchored to
        // the evidence it describes.
        created_time: time,
        desc: f"{_break} on record {metadata.uid} of {$scope_word} {attestation_list[0].chain_uid}",
      },
      unmapped: {
        break: _break,
        chain_uid: attestation_list[0].chain_uid,
        record_uid: metadata.uid,
        record_time: time,
        stored_fingerprint: attestation_list[0]?.fingerprint?.value?,
        computed_fingerprint: _recomputed,
        prev_event_fingerprint: _link_att?.prev_event?.fingerprint?.value?,
        predecessor_fingerprint: _prev?.fp? if _prev? != null else null,
      },
    }
  }
}
